WEBVTT

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.060
The Middle Ages.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:28.190
A war-torn period in which European countries
were in conflict over religions and customs.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:32.400
The people named their own staple food, bread, after their native lands,

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.740
and used it to measure its origin.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.280
English bread, German bread, French bread,

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.430
and other breads bearing their country’s name were born.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.630
Now, in the 21st century, on a small island country in Asia...

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:52.400
Even here in Japan, there is a boy attempting
to create a unique bread.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.020
But in order to do that...

00:00:59.220 --> 00:00:59.950
Yeah!

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.690
It’s finally complete!

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.470
he needed Solar Hands.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.560
Jii-chan, Baa-chan, have some of this.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:08.980
My latest...

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:09.780
Huh?

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.340
Where’d everyone go?

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:15.700
It’s a burglar! Save me!

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:16.540
Kazuma, this isn’t the time to be baking bread.
It’s a burglar! Save me!

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.450
Kazuma, this isn’t the time to be baking bread.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.740
We’re going to be late for the bullet train.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.410
S-Sis!

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.410
Oh! That’s right.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:27.000
I was going to Tokyo today.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.060
Jeez, you’re so troublesome.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.820
My bread! My bread!

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:35.450
I-I’m scared!

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.760
We’re just taking a little shortcut.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:39.870
Hold on tight!

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:57.260
Trees!

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.010
Watch out!

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.470
I’m sorry, I’m in a hurry.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.510
I’m going to die!

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:22.330
Sorry to keep you waiting.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.330
Ah, they’re here.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.180
What were you doing at a time like this?

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.140
He was just calmly baking bread.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:31.830
I’m alive, somehow.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.610
It’s freshly baked, people.
Please have some.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.320
Oh, a new creation?

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.950
It looks like regular an-pan though.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.420
Delicious!

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.980
It’s delicious, better than #54.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.200
I wrapped the azuki you cooked
for me in a special bread.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.930
The natural sweetness of the wheat
and anko are blending gracefully.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.740
I saw Japan for an instant!

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:08.730
Note: “Japan” is a pun, a combination of “Japanese” and “pan,” which means bread.
Well of course, the bread I make
is Japanese bread, <i>Japan</i>.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.960
This is <i>Japan</i> #55!

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.310
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.190
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.690
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:23.030
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:28.160
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:31.630
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.490
dokoka tooku de mimi wo sumashiteru hito ga iru
Somewhere far away, there are people listening carefully.

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:49.080
Yakitate!! Japan
dokoka tooku de mimi wo sumashiteru hito ga iru
Somewhere far away, there are people listening carefully.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.410
Yakitate!! Japan

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:50.420
arayuru basho de sora wo miageteru hito ga iru
In every place, there are people looking up at the sky.
Yakitate!! Japan

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:57.050
In every place, there are people looking up at the sky.
arayuru basho de sora wo miageteru hito ga iru

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:04.930
yozora no shita de kuchibue fuiteru bokutachi wa
Under the night sky, we, who are whistling...

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:12.640
kotoba mo nai mama yubi de tada seiza wo nazotteru
without words, are just tracing constellations with our fingers.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:20.110
samugariya no yume tsumetai kimi no te
The dreams of the cold-sensitive... Your cold hands...

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:29.490
atatameru mahou wa hitotsu no michi wo shinjiru koto
The warming magic is to believe in one path.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:38.250
houkigumo no mukou ni mitsuketa hitotsubu no hoshi wa
The single star found on the other side of the comet cloud...

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:42.510
kagayaku hoshi demo kasukana hoshi demo
whether it be a shining or a faint star...

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.470
kimi dake no hikari
is the light of only you.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.250
mune no kumo no mukou ni mienai mama no michishirube
On the other side of the cloud in your heart,

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:54.060
is a sign post that remains unseen.
mune no kumo no mukou ni mienai mama no michishirube

00:04:54.520 --> 00:05:01.780
saa kono te wo hiraite ima nani wo shinjimasuka?
Now, opening these hands, what will you believe in today?

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:09.080
He has arrived!!
The boy who possesses
Solar Hands!

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.030
Well, I’ll be going then.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.190
You’re really going away.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:17.110
To become a baker immediately after
graduating from middle school...

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:18.580
You’re a curious grandson.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:23.910
But to think he would be employed by a really
famous bakery that competes with the best,

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.790
it’s incredible! Yeah!

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:27.830
If you’re sent back with complaints,

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:29.130
I’ll beat you!

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:29.850
O-okay.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:32.310
Kazuma,

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.960
this is from me.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:36.310
Thank you, Baa-chan.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:41.140
This really is goodbye...
to your warm hands, too.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:46.450
Niigata

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:46.570
Time flies, doesn’t it?
Niigata

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:47.730
Time flies, doesn’t it?

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:51.860
It’s been about 10 years since
our breakfast became bread.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:55.650
Yep.

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:57.620
That’s something which happened 10 years ago.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.920
It was back when I, Azuma Kazuma,
was only six years old.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:05.200
Mama.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.620
That’s displeasing, call me Okaa-chan.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.760
Okaa-chan, I’d like some bread for a change.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:13.270
Don’t utter such foolishness! You’re asking
a rice farmer to have bread for breakfast?!

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:15.760
Our family’s the only one still saying that.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.050
At my classmate Manami-chan’s place,
they all eat bread with their meals.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.840
Well, they grow wheat there, too.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.310
That isn’t the problem, Baa-chan!

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.280
Say, Kazuma, what do you think?

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.390
I like the rice we grow here.

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.280
Bread is all dried-out, I don’t like it.

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:34.160
Oh?

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.230
I’m going to die!

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:38.440
Nee-chan, I’m going to die!

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.990
You only say that because you’ve
never eaten tasty bread.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:46.420
I’ll make you try some real bread now, so be prepared!

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:51.090
I’ve fallen, I’ve fallen, Onee-chan!

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:55.140
Oh?

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:01.180
Huh, was there a place like this here?

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:02.650
Come on! Hurry up and stand.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:09.320
It’s a windmill!

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:10.820
And a reindeer!

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.320
It’s like...

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.030
a toy store.

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:21.990
Those are all edible.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.000
Because they’re bread.

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.240
Good morning, Mister!

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.130
Good morning.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:30.720
This is my little brother, Kazuma.

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:33.220
Welcome, Kazuma-kun.

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:35.220
Y-Yeah.

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:37.670
But I really am surprised...

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:39.730
All of these are made of bread.

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:40.920
Mister,

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:42.590
I’d like this dinner bread.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:44.230
Thank you for your business.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:46.650
Kazuma-kun.

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:47.730
This is...

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:49.860
the taste of...

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:51.360
real bread!

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:00.790
Ouch!

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:03.290
That was delicious!

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:05.160
Ouch...

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:06.410
You understand now, right?

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:08.800
The reason why I want our daily food to be bread.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:13.290
How do you make such tasty bread?

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:16.300
Would you like to watch me make bread?

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:18.760
Really? Is that okay?

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:21.260
Inaho-chan, what about school?

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:25.440
I’m going to be late!
Please take care of this kid!

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:29.680
Want to see?

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:30.440
Yeah!

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:32.950
Okay?

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:37.350
Flour and sugar, salt, butter, yeast germ, water,

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:40.030
non-fat milk, and milk all are mixed together.

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:43.440
If starts off sticky,

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:46.200
but after about five minutes, gluten
is produced, and it settles.

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.240
After kneading it for 12 to 15 minutes,

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:53.920
you round it.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:59.500
You let this ferment until it expands
to many times its size.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.090
What’s “ferment?”

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.230
Well, it’s a bit complicated, but...

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:07.970
It’s when microbes such as yeast and
bacteria break down organic matter,

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:10.020
producing carbon dioxide, which causes it to expand.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:16.140
Would you like to try it up to this stage?

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:17.530
Yeah, let’s do it!

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:22.110
Hey, you have strong muscles.

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:25.790
By the way, your Onee-san seems
to like bread, but what about you?

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:26.820
Yeah!

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.700
I just started liking it.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:31.300
But I still prefer rice.

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:34.260
You’re a very outspoken boy.

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:36.790
I’m an honest person!

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:38.510
I see, I see. I’m sorry.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:41.670
To tell you the truth,
I’m a rice man, too.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:43.020
I love rice.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:45.960
What? But this is a bakery!

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.130
Then why did you become a baker?

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:49.980
Well, let’s see...

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:54.860
Rice tastes good to eat,
but bread is fun to make.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:01.900
For Japanese people, I don’t think bread can beat rice as the staple.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:05.370
But one day, I’d like to create a bread that surpasses rice in taste.

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:08.990
Namely, <i>Japan</i>.

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:11.620
A Japanese bread that can be presented
to the world proudly.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:14.090
To create that is my dream.

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:18.000
Bread that tastes better than rice... <i>Japan</i>.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.640
I want to eat it!

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:21.840
But although I say that...

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:25.380
Before that dream is realized,

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.600
this shop may be gone.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:32.640
I studied a lot of things in France,

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:36.150
and succeeded well as an artisan over there.

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:37.850
But you know,

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:39.980
to make the <i>Japan</i> dream come true,

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.480
I had to return to Japan.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:45.650
Thinking I would make my hometown my destination,

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:48.650
I set up store here, which was fine...

00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:51.330
But due to the nature of this
locality, my bread won’t sell!

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.090
They’re this tasty, but they won’t sell?

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:59.710
Although telling you these things won’t do much good...

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:01.370
Would you like some juice or something...?

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:02.510
Leave it to me.

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:05.270
Leave it to me!

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:09.720
I will get Jii-chan to like bread,
and increase your customers.

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:12.230
I’ll give him your bread.

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:14.480
K-Kazuma-kun...

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.020
Customers, huh?

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:22.360
I’m grateful, but truthfully,
this store is already...

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:25.230
Oh?

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:28.910
This is the dough Kazuma-kun
was kneading until just now...

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:33.360
It can’t be...

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:34.710
That boy’s hands are...

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:42.160
What’s this?!

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:44.550
What happened to my rice?! My rice?!

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.810
Kazuma and Inaho...

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.050
they got us good.

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:49.010
Jii-chan!

00:11:55.270 --> 00:11:56.600
This is toast!

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:58.560
Please try it, even if only once!

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:00.230
I promise it’ll taste good.

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:02.600
Kazuma...

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:05.240
this was all Inoha’s idea, right?

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:08.120
You really like rice, don’t you?

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:09.370
Eat it!

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:12.920
Kazuma has betrayed me...

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:20.920
Hieko-san, give me my usual nattou and miso soup.

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:22.170
Jii-chan!

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:26.170
Don’t get me wrong, grandchildren!
I’ll eat the toast or whatever.

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:29.930
But I cannot part with the nattou and
miso soup that I have every morning!

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:39.970
They don’t go together!

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:41.560
Of course they don’t!

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:44.140
Your dream of toast is still just a dream.

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:45.450
No way...

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:48.980
If you really must make toast our breakfast,

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:51.650
then bring me toast that goes
with nattou and miso soup!

00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:52.700
End of story!

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:08.500
Toast that goes with nattou and miso soup...
That’s definitely impossible.

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:10.750
Jii-chan is a rice lover by nature.

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:12.470
Baa-chan said so.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:16.000
At the end of the war, the supplies from the American army

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:19.590
were just lousy hardtacks and rolls.

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:23.430
To be able to fill his stomach with rice again someday,

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:26.740
that was Jii-chan’s dream.

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:28.650
Because he had such feelings,

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:33.150
the rice grown from Jii-chan’s rice fields is so delicious.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:36.080
To give bread to that Jii-chan...

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.740
Toast that goes with nattou
and miso soup...?

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:41.320
It might not be impossible.

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:42.450
What?

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:43.450
You can do it?

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:46.220
To begin with,
bread is purposefully designed...

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:50.330
to have the potential to go with any given type of meal.

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:54.390
The idea, and love, would create
the ultimate hidden taste.

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:57.480
Kazuma-kun.

00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:00.930
You will create <i>Japan</i> for your ojii-chan.

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:02.430
I will create <i>Japan</i>?

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:05.690
Yes. <i>Japan</i> that people like your ojii-chan will approve of.

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:10.740
I want to test how far my bread will go.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:14.110
But for that, I need your power.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:17.540
I’ll do it!

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:18.950
Good.

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:20.740
Way to go, Kazuma.

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:22.750
By the way, Inaho-chan,
what about school?

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:26.800
Late again!
I’ll leave the rest to you, Mister!

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:33.810
Flour and sugar, salt, egg, butter,
yeast germ, water, non-fat milk...

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:35.310
and then...

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:38.560
let’s put this in, instead of milk.

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:40.430
What’s that?

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:42.060
A little hidden taste.

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:45.020
Now, Kazuma-kun, knead it
with all you’ve got.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:46.560
Right!

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:08.050
The fermentation’s speed is different
from normal, just as I thought.

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:10.040
There’s no mistaking it.

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:12.630
Kazuma-kun possesses <i>those</i> hands.

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:16.170
Even amongst the first-class bread artisans of France,

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:17.670
they’re said to be very rare.

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.100
Those magical hands.

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:22.020
This kid has those hands...

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:26.180
Come on, give it some more work.

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:27.260
Okay!

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:28.900
That’s good! Just like that.

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:35.610
Split the dough into four pieces,
and put them in that pan.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:37.200
Try not to let it lose its shape.

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:41.440
After putting it in the proofer like
this for final fermentation,

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:44.820
bake it in a 220°C oven for 45 minutes, then it’s done.

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:46.420
Wow.

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:49.630
This is splendid, Kazuma-kun.

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:52.630
With your hands,
a miracle is sure to happen.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:00.520
I don’t see any change.

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:04.020
I’d rather not remember such a horrible taste.

00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:06.550
Hieko-san, white rice...

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:08.020
Just eat it!

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:09.230
Very well.

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:14.160
If this time tastes so bad again that I feel
like dying, I won’t hear of it ever again.

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:17.240
I will not forgive any mention of
the word “bread” around here!

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:18.740
Fine!

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:25.080
God, be with me...

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:36.670
T-This can’t be...

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:48.820
I ate nattou, and I approve!

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:50.310
Mito Nattou
I ate nattou, and I approve!

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:50.320
Mito Nattou

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:53.950
Mizoshirumunmatau kaminomizoshiru...

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:56.280
Oh my god!

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:03.950
Test... Toast!

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:06.200
Watch out!

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:14.920
Nattou miso soup!

00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:16.930
Here you are!

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:31.650
Not half bad.

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:33.180
It’s not bad!

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:35.030
What’s going on?

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:37.110
We did it!

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:39.200
Yeah!

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.270
Why does it taste so good?

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:44.690
Of course it tastes good!

00:17:44.690 --> 00:17:47.710
Because it’s bread made especially for you, Jii-chan.

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:49.700
Kazuma...

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:52.120
For... me...

00:17:58.580 --> 00:17:59.970
Kazuma...

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:08.220
Mister!

00:18:10.430 --> 00:18:12.060
Hey, Kazuma-kun.

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:15.810
Well, that’s really great.

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:17.890
Your ojii-chan was happy, wasn’t he?

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:18.990
Yeah!

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:23.570
By the way, what was that stuff
you put in instead of milk?

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:26.540
That was soymilk.

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:28.250
Soymilk?

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:33.200
Nattou, miso soup, and soymilk, too...
They’re all made from the soybean.

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:35.630
That’s why the matching was perfect.

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:37.330
I see.

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:38.210
But you know...

00:18:38.870 --> 00:18:42.310
What made it possible to bring out so much
good flavor from the soymilk toast...

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:44.590
were those magical hands of yours.

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:49.420
It’s because you have Solar Hands.

00:18:49.420 --> 00:18:51.270
“Solar Hands?”

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:55.190
You see, bread doesn’t ferment unless it’s warm.

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:59.320
So, the temperature inside the
shop is set to about 30°C.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:01.410
But you know...

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:03.860
No matter how superb the air conditioning
equipment you use to warm it is,

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:05.950
the temperature of a person’s hands doesn’t change.

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:08.190
That’s why, at the center of production in France,

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:11.830
they call warm hands like yours
Solar Hands.

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:15.030
Kazuma-kun,

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:16.920
I’ve decided to go to Tokyo.

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:18.420
H-Huh?

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:22.000
In order to give birth to <i>Japan</i>, and
spread it all over the country,

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:23.930
it has to be done in Tokyo, after all.

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:26.930
When I returned from France...

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:31.600
To be honest, I didn’t have the confidence to
manage in Tokyo, so I ran to my hometown.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:36.230
I left something precious behind
in order to come back, too...

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.560
But, it’s different now.

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:41.730
Watching you try so hard...

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:44.410
I cannot lose, either.
That’s what I thought.

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:49.000
Will I... ever see you again?

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:51.490
Well...

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:55.450
If you aspire to be a baker from now on,

00:19:55.450 --> 00:19:59.510
and are able to continue pursuing
your dream of <i>Japan</i>...

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:03.760
we may be able to meet again one day.

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:07.630
10 years have passed...

00:20:08.810 --> 00:20:13.470
To tell the truth, I don’t exactly
remember his face anymore.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:18.320
However, those words I still
remember clearly, even now.

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:22.270
And I’m still chasing after my <i>Japan</i>.

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:24.640
Soymilk bread was...

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:27.070
my <i>Japan</i> creation #1.

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:29.770
Producing one after another since that time,

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:31.660
I’ve now reached #55.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:34.650
Here, at the center of Japan, Tokyo,

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:37.160
I’ll start with #56.

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:40.630
My battle to find the new <i>Japan</i> will begin!

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:43.040
No matter how many if takes before I’m finished...

00:20:43.040 --> 00:20:45.170
I’ll definitely make it!

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:50.770
Whoa!

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:53.170
That’s a huge store!

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:54.730
This is Japan’s number one bakery...

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:57.060
Pantasia!

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:00.230
Okay!

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:01.930
Hello!

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:03.720
I’m counting on you from today on!

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.410
I’m Azuma Kazuma.

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:07.060
Azuma Kazuma, eh?

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:08.520
You’re five minutes late.

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:10.500
O-Oh... I’m sorry.

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:12.360
Five point deduction.

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:13.250
Huh?

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:14.990
Late, apparently.

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:17.110
That guy’s already down to half, and
hasn’t even done anything yet.

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:18.250
I guess it’s over for him.

00:21:18.250 --> 00:21:19.750
Moron.

00:21:19.750 --> 00:21:21.500
I’ll beat him.

00:21:21.950 --> 00:21:23.740
He came all this way, too...

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:25.510
It’s unfortunate.

00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:27.250
Got it, people?

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:29.250
This examination uses a point deduction system.

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:30.840
You begin with 10 points.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:34.230
If you lose all your points, you’ll be disqualified.

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:37.220
If you’re disqualified, gather your
belongings and go home.

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:37.980
Understand?

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:40.980
What the...?

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:45.640
In that case, we’ll begin the Pantasia main
store’s new employee examination.

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:47.480
All of you, line up on one side.

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:48.600
What are you doing?

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:50.900
Don’t be slow, kid with only five points.

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:54.700
Huh?

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:59.610
I’m the person in charge of this
examination, Kuroyanagi Ryou.

00:21:59.610 --> 00:22:02.120
Pantasia’s Tokyo main store new employee recruitment

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:04.050
will be decided by the trials to follow.

00:22:04.580 --> 00:22:07.830
However, there will be only one
chosen out of the 35 here.

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:08.710
One?

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:11.550
Whoa, this is really serious.

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:14.500
But oh well, I’ll just do my best
until the end.

00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:15.310
Yeah!

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:19.520
That confidence...

00:22:20.090 --> 00:22:21.640
Perhaps that person...

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:24.900
has the legendary Solar Hands...

00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:27.950
I won’t lose!

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:29.980
I’ll fight with <i>Japan</i>!

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:32.150
Azuma Kazuma’s battle for <i>Japan</i>...

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:33.780
begins now!

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:46.630
If I think of you, and even utter the clichéd phrase “I love you,”

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:55.640
it makes such a nice sound, and you continue talking.

00:22:59.210 --> 00:23:06.650
The hopeless tears your bold self sometimes shows me form flag of truce.

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:11.860
If nothing is said, then all the more,

00:23:12.020 --> 00:23:17.960
I just can’t look away. Things like sadness and pain...

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:20.530
Well, that happens a lot in life, after all.

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:24.670
When you casually say, “Do your best,” it gives me strength.

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:29.440
Whenever your small back trembles, I will embrace you right away.

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:33.280
Even if the scenery outside my window changes...

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:37.980
Stay with me as you are, bad sleeping habits and all.

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:43.150
If you sulk, and utter

00:23:43.420 --> 00:23:51.960
Even the clichéd “I hate you,” it comes across as the opposite.

00:23:52.360 --> 00:24:01.320
I will love you, no matter what. It’s just another Sunday to me.

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:08.910
In that case, we’ll begin the Pantasia main
store’s new employee examination.

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:10.410
I’ll fight with <i>Japan</i>!

00:24:10.410 --> 00:24:11.750
What’s <i>Japan</i>?

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:13.460
Is this guy gonna be okay?

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:15.540
Next episode, Yakitate!! Japan.

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:18.340
Maharajah!! The day Mount Fuji fell!

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:19.420
Be sure to see it!

00:24:19.970 --> 00:24:22.720
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:23.140 --> 00:24:25.550
Slices of bread are not counted with “ikko” or “niko”.

00:24:25.550 --> 00:24:27.600
We count them with “ikkin” and “nikin.”

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:31.730
“Kin” has been used since the time of
ancient China, and is a unit of weight!

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:33.980
Wow!
